  "source": {
    "title": "Belfast News-Letter, 22 May 1964, p. 12 (\"Mayor of Lurgan is among the election casualties\")",
    "publisher": "Belfast News-Letter (via the British Newspaper Archive)",
    "kind": "news",
    "check": "every printed figure read from the page image; A. H. C. Greer's 2,367 is derived from the report that he lost \"by only 15 votes\" to the twelfth elected (H. D. Gregg, 2,382)"
  },
  "note": "Election of the twelve councillors, polled Wednesday 20 May 1964 (the three aldermen elected in 1961 remained in office). Twenty-one candidates; 74.66 per cent poll. Eight of the twelve official Unionist nominees were elected; four Roman Catholic members of the Independent Citizens' Association (I.C.A.) won seats, Dr Donnell Deeny topping the poll. The Mayor, A. H. C. Greer (official Unionist), was defeated; Mrs Winifred Monroe, a former Mayor, stood as an Independent and came sixteenth with 1,891; Jim Maginnis, the official Northern Ireland Labour nominee, had 1,805. The paper's \"full results\" list omits Greer, Monroe and Maginnis, whose figures are given in its text."
}
